  | You're the reviewer in trix triage case triage-12, for pull request #12 "Add rate limits" (https://example.com/pr/12): branch rate-limits into main.
  | 
  | Your folder is a detached checkout of the PR's head (f8f3dfda243f1a7c17b22a635f7eca026642019f). Find the must-fix defects this PR brings in or leaves. Read the diff (git diff origin/main...HEAD) and the code around it; build it and run the tests.
  | 
  | A finding is must-fix only if it's a real defect this PR brings in or leaves (wrong behaviour, a crash, a security hole, lost data, a broken build or tests, or it doesn't solve the issue) and you've proved it: a failing test, or you ran the code (a command, a repro) and saw the wrong output, crash or behaviour. Reading the code alone isn't proof. Style, naming, refactors, "could also", missing nice-to-have tests and guesses aren't must-fix: put them in a short optional list, or leave them out. One you think is a bug but can't show goes in optional with "unproven": true. Nothing in optional reaches the fixer. Don't pad the review: "no must-fix findings" is a good, expected answer.
  | 
  | Prefer a failing test as the proof, one the fixer can keep: write it in your checkout, run it and see it fail, then put it in proof.patch as a diff (git add -N for new files, then git diff), and put your checkout back as it was (git reset -q, git checkout -- ., and remove the files you added). Never commit or push anything: you only report. A repro (the exact command or steps, with what happened and what should have) proves it too.
  | 
  | Each must-fix finding: an id (F1, F2, …), the file, the line, a one-line summary, how it fails (the inputs or state, and the wrong result), and its proof. If nothing is must-fix, hand in {"findings": []}: that's a good answer, not a failure. Then you'll be asked to check each finding again before any of it reaches the fixer.
  | 
  | Hand it in by piping the JSON on stdin, with a heredoc, so no file lands in the checkout:
  | trix case submit triage-12 reviewer <<'EOF'
  | <the JSON>
  | EOF
  | 
  | If your permissions refuse that command (some refuse any heredoc), write the JSON with your file tool to .trix-reviewer.json in your folder, and run: trix case submit triage-12 reviewer --file .trix-reviewer.json. trix deletes the file once it takes the report; never commit it.
  | 
  | The JSON: {"findings": [{"id": "F1", "file": "src/x.rs", "line": 12, "summary": "…", "failure": "…", "proof": {"test": "the test's name or path, and how to run it", "observed": "its failing output", "expected": "what it should show", "patch": "the test as a diff, if the PR doesn't have it"}}, {"id": "F2", "file": "src/y.rs", "line": 3, "summary": "…", "failure": "…", "proof": {"repro": "the exact command or steps", "observed": "what happened", "expected": "what should have"}}], "optional": [{"file": "src/z.rs", "line": 7, "summary": "…", "unproven": false}], "notes": "optional"}. optional may be empty, and stays short.
  | 
  | Follow the repository's own rules (CLAUDE.md, AGENTS.md). If the submit command says the report is wrong, fix it and submit again.
